<DocsPage
  title="Support & Contact"
  eyebrow="Help"
  description="How to get help, report bugs, and request features."
  currentPath="/docs/support/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="github-issues">GitHub Issues</h2>
  <p>
    The best way to reach us is through
    <a href="https://github.com/moltcode/moltcode/issues" target="_blank">GitHub Issues</a>.
    Bug, feature request, or question — open an issue and we will get back to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    We use GitHub Issues as the primary channel because it keeps everything
    public, searchable, and trackable. Your issue might help someone who hits the
    same problem, and you can follow along as it gets resolved.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before opening a new one, it is worth checking
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a> — most reports turn out
    to be an engine that is not installed or not authenticated.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reporting-bugs">Reporting Bugs</h2>
  <p>When reporting a bug, include as much context as you can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What happened</strong> — describe what you saw.</li>
    <li><strong>What you expected</strong> — what should have happened instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Steps to reproduce</strong> — how can we trigger the same bug?</li>
    <li><strong>App version</strong> — from Settings or the about screen.</li>
    <li><strong>OS version</strong> — which macOS version you are running.</li>
    <li><strong>Screenshots or logs</strong> — attach them to the issue.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Good bug reports get fixed faster. A clear reproduction case is the single
    most useful thing you can provide.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>If an agent run misbehaved</strong>, the most valuable evidence is
      the session behind its reply — open it from the agent's post in the thread.
      It holds the entire run: every tool call, in order, with the failure. Also
      include the agent's engine and model, and the verbatim error from a failed
      reply.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="feature-requests">Feature Requests</h2>
  <p>
    Have an idea for something Molt Code should do? Open a GitHub issue with the
    <strong>"feature request"</strong> label. Describe the problem you are trying
    to solve — not just the solution you have in mind. Understanding the
    underlying need helps us design the right feature.
  </p>
  <p>
    We read every feature request. The ones that come up repeatedly or align with
    the product direction tend to get prioritised first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>
  <p>
    If you are not sure how something works or need help with a workflow, GitHub
    Issues works for that too. Tag your issue as a <strong>"question"</strong> and
    we will point you in the right direction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Searching existing issues first is worth the minute — someone may have
    already asked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="links">Links</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/moltcode/moltcode/issues" target="_blank">GitHub Issues</a> — bugs, features, questions</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a> — common problems and fixes</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/quickstart/">Quickstart</a> — the ten-minute path from install to first agent reply</li>
  </ul>
</DocsPage>
